e SAME lifted clock fails the assertion proper — two different classes, proven apart. ⭐ **AUTHORED + DISPATCHED: `f1630-1-m1-06-weighting-timeout` (lane-a) — and its whole design problem is that THE RUNNER CANNOT SEE THIS RED.** The lane shell passes the test at 30 s; that same runner scored the suite 24/24 there. So a before/after cure claim would be **vacuous or invented** (the F-1590-1 / F-1592-1 class), and the master says so in a dedicated section: it FORBIDS the cure claim, FORBIDS the measured-unreliable CPU-hog lever, and instead asks the runner to (a) prove the **LEVER** by manufacturing a sub-duration timeout, (b) report the lane-shell durations the finding lacks, and (c) state plainly what its shell cannot verify. **The acceptance measurement is assigned to the DRAIN, which runs where the red lives.** Firewall also bars the 40/40/40/20 sample sizes (shrinking them would weaken the ratio assertion under cover of a perf fix) and the GLOBAL timeout (which would hand every test in the suite a longer leash to hide in). Dispatch order obeyed per F-1424-3: master+leaf+row committed FIRST (`6b757dd50`), **a citation fix committed SECOND (`78b9ab9e4`) because my own draft cited the assertion at `:200` when it is at `:201` — the very fire documenting citation rot wrote a coordinate one line off, caught by measuring instead of trusting the draft**, lane refreshed THIRD, **all 9 cited coordinates + the SEQUENCING key verified 1:1 on main AND in the refreshed lane** FOURTH, pre-queue `--strict --queue` gate CLEAR, `cp` last. 🟡 **F-1630-2 (advisory, no task owed):** the repair hardcodes the cap `3` at four sites and names `Balance.upgrades.doubleTapCoilMaxStacks` only in comments, so a future cap move breaks the same three surfaces and makes the comments wrong rather than merely stale. **Not a criticism of the runner** — the master firewalled it to fixture-only, and obeying a firewall outranks improving a design. For whoever next opens that file with a wider mandate. 🧾