r had teeth.** `test:desk-birth` is excused on the identical false sentence, but `package.json:26` chains `npm run test:desk-birth` → `node scripts/desk-birth-guard.mjs` **live**, so that guard has run against the real board the whole time. `desk-carryforward-guard.mjs` was genuinely the only post-bookkeeping guard resting on the law claim **alone** — s1666's discriminator was right, and it is now right *by measurement* over the whole population instead of over a sample of two. ⚠️ **BUT THE LIVE HAZARD IT LEFT IS REAL AND IS WHAT I ACTUALLY CURED: the false sentence is still the FIRST reason a reader meets in that entry, and the cure is a single line in `package.json` with nothing next to it explaining why it is load-bearing.** A later fire doing tidy-up reads *"Its caller is a LAW (scripts/fire.md §4)"*, concludes the npm wiring is redundant belt-and-braces, deletes it — and F-1666-2 reopens silently, with the guard once again passing its own unit tests against fixtures while never seeing `STATUS.md`. **That is not a hypothetical reading; it is the exact reading the entry was written to support.** ➡️ **CURED IN THE FILE, SUPERSEDE-NOT-ERASE PER THE RETENTION LAW:** both entries keep their original sentence verbatim as the record of the defect, each now followed by the measurement that refutes it, the TRUE caller (`package.json:26`, wired s1666 at `6ed1a1c32`), and for `desk-carryforward` an explicit **DO NOT remove that wiring**. The two are deliberately annotated *differently* so a later reader cannot conflate a load-bearing false claim with a decorative one. ✓ `gate-caller-audit` re-run after the edit: **PASS**, 23 grandfathered, owner escalations 3, unrouted 0 — the entries stay correct and stay needed, because reachability is computed from ROOTS and `test:ledger-guards` is deliberately unrooted. 🚫 **NO GUARD PROPOSED, AND THE REASON IS THE MEASUREMENT, NOT A PREFERENCE.** A caller-claim checker is genuinely mechanisable — the claims name a file and the key is a